  "report": "# E007 split-pod KV (CPU part): COMPLETED\n\nSeeds: 17, 29, 43, 71, 101. Every seed must hold every coverage and falsifier rule.\n\n- seed 17: valid; 0 coverage rules unmet, 0 falsifier rules tripped\n- seed 29: valid; 0 coverage rules unmet, 0 falsifier rules tripped\n- seed 43: valid; 0 coverage rules unmet, 0 falsifier rules tripped\n- seed 71: valid; 0 coverage rules unmet, 0 falsifier rules tripped\n- seed 101: valid; 0 coverage rules unmet, 0 falsifier rules tripped\n\n- mutation check: 59 of 59 killed\n\nLimitations: the code under test is the CPU reference path on a tiny seeded model: bit-identity checks the plumbing (the stage hand-over, framing, reassembly), not the math, because the layer code is shared by the split and the single-pod run; no real checkpoint, no GPU, no other dtype than F32; frames cross the real PodWire request frame codec in process: no socket, no iroh transport, no second host, no latency or throughput claim (E008); the chunk envelope is authenticated with an Ed25519 signature of the upstream runtime registered per authenticated principal; key distribution, rotation and the binding of the principal to a connection are not modelled; the time is a virtual clock the harness advances; clock skew is a tolerance, not a simulated drift; a completed frame holds one of 64 table slots per principal until its signed deadline plus the skew bound, so one principal sustains at most 64 / (deadline budget + skew) frames per second; the cases stay inside that bound by signing deadlines 100 ms ahead; the extensions of the design (input lineage and raw ancestry, Approximate frames and taint, persistence rejection, the expert, adapter, base-model, semantic, memory and derived kinds of the invalidation matrix) are E008's; the restore path does not apply because no KV is restored on a Pod; synthetic seeded token sequences; one process, one machine profile.\n"
